    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg.jpg" alt="Pillar of Eliseg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elisegs_Pfeiler_5_S%C3%BCdost.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Powys emerged in the fifth century in the lands of the Cornovii, a British people whose Roman civitas capital was Viroconium, the town now called Wroxeter near Shrewsbury. When Roman government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> ended around 410, local elites took over authority in the region, and excavations at Wroxeter show large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a> buildings raised on the site of the baths basilica after Roman rule. The name Powys is often derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> <em>pagenses</em>, ‘country people’, although the etymology is not certain. The dynasty that later ruled the kingdom traced its descent from Cadell Ddyrnllug, and the ninth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a> near Llangollen linked the royal line to Vortigern and to Magnus Maximus, the Roman usurper of 383–388; these claims show how later kings wished to be seen rather than reliable history. The early kingdom probably stretched from the Welsh uplands across the upper Severn valley and into the plains of what became Shropshire and Cheshire. Its first royal centre may have been at Wroxeter or at Pengwern, a place celebrated in later Welsh poetry whose site is unknown and has often been placed at or near Shrewsbury. Most of its early history rests on later genealogies and poems.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hywel-dda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hywel-dda.jpg" alt="Hywel Dda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyweldda_historieofcambria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Humphrey Llwyd, David Powell, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Powys was a land of upland pasture, wooded river valleys and, on its eastern side, good lowland farmland along the Severn and the Dee. Its wealth lay mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, which served as the measure of value in fines, dowries and tribute, together with sheep, pigs and cereals such as oats and barley grown in the valleys. Society was organised by kindred: free landholding families, whose rights rested on inherited shares in the land, owed renders of food and hospitality to the king, while unfree tenants worked royal and noble estates. Welsh law, later associated with the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>, set compensation for killing and insult through payments known as <em>galanas</em> and <em>sarhaed</em>, divided land among male heirs and defined the duties of the royal household. Towns were almost absent after Wroxeter declined, and trade was small in scale, although cattle were driven across the border to English markets. The church was strong and local in character: Meifod in the Vyrnwy valley, associated with Saint Tysilio, was the principal church of the kingdom and a royal burial place, and many small churches bore the names of local saints. The royal court was also a centre of Welsh poetry, which reached a high point in the twelfth-century praise poems of Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr for Madog ap Maredudd.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Offa of Mercia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offa,_King_of_Mercia,_silver_penny;_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Offa (07..-0796 ; roi de Mercie). Autorité émettrice de monnaie, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Warfare in Powys was waged by the king’s household war band, the <em>teulu</em>, and by levies of free men, fighting mostly on foot with spears and shields, with nobles on horseback. For three centuries the kingdom’s main enemy was the expanding English kingdom of Mercia, together with Northumbria. Around 615 Æthelfrith of Northumbria defeated the Britons at the Battle of Chester, where King Selyf ap Cynan of Powys was killed and, according to Bede, about 1,200 monks of Bangor-on-Dee who had come to pray for the British army were slaughtered. During the seventh century the lowlands of Shropshire passed into English hands, a loss mourned in later Welsh poetry about Cynddylan and the burning of his hall at Pengwern. In the eighth century Elisedd ap Gwylog won back land from the English, as his great-grandson’s pillar records, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a> (r. 757–796) built Offa’s Dyke, an earthwork running for well over 100 kilometres along the frontier with Powys and its neighbours, probably to mark and control the border. In 822, according to the <em>Annales Cambriae</em>, the Mercians took the region of Powys ‘into their power’. Later rulers, facing the Norman lords of the March, built castles of their own: Madog ap Maredudd raised a castle at Oswestry in about 1149.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rhodri-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great.jpg" alt="Rhodri the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodri_Mawr_-_Historiae_Cambriae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Powell, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Powys was ruled by kings drawn from a royal kindred, and succession was often contested among sons, brothers and cousins, since Welsh custom did not give the eldest son a firm right to the throne. The king moved between his courts, or <em>llysoedd</em>, in the cantrefi and commotes of the kingdom, receiving food renders and dispensing justice with the help of court officers such as the <em>penteulu</em>, the captain of the household troop, and the court judge. The old dynasty ended with Cyngen ap Cadell, who raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a> and died on pilgrimage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> in 854. Powys is then usually said to have passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> of Gwynedd, whose mother Nest is traditionally named as Cyngen’s sister, and for two centuries it was usually governed by kings based outside it, the last and greatest of them Gruffudd ap Llywelyn (d. 1063). After Gruffudd’s death in 1063, Bleddyn ap Cynfyn and his brother Rhiwallon were set up as rulers of Gwynedd and Powys under the overlordship of Edward the Confessor, and Bleddyn’s descendants made Powys a separate kingdom again. Later medieval lawbooks credited Bleddyn with amending Welsh law. Under Maredudd ap Bleddyn (d. 1132) and his son Madog ap Maredudd, Powys was ruled from courts traditionally centred on Mathrafal near Meifod.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#owain-glyndwr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr.jpg" alt="Owain Glyndŵr" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Owain_Glynd%C5%B5r_portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jr8825, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Powys never dominated Wales, but it shaped the long frontier between the Welsh and the English. Its loss of the lowlands between the Severn and the Dee helped to fix the boundary that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>’s Dyke later marked, close to the modern border of Wales and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a>, set up by Cyngen ap Cadell near the site of the later Valle Crucis Abbey, is one of the most important inscribed monuments of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>: its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> text gave a royal genealogy and recalled Germanus, Vortigern and Magnus Maximus, revealing the stories with which Welsh rulers claimed a Roman and British past. The cycles of verse known as <em>Canu Llywarch Hen</em> and <em>Canu Heledd</em>, associated with Powys and probably composed in the ninth or tenth century, are among the finest early Welsh poetry and turned the defeats of the seventh century into lasting literature. In the twelfth century Madog ap Maredudd patronised Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr, one of the greatest court poets of medieval Wales, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#owain-cyfeiliog">Owain Cyfeiliog</a>, Madog’s nephew and ruler of southern Powys, was himself a poet to whom the poem <em>Hirlas Owain</em> is attributed. Through the line of Madog ap Maredudd, the princes of northern Powys also gave rise to the lords of Glyndyfrdwy, from whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, leader of the Welsh revolt of 1400, descended.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Powys first collapsed in the ninth century: after the Mercian conquest recorded in 822 and Cyngen’s death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> in 854 the kingdom lost its own dynasty and was absorbed into the lands ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> and his descendants from Gwynedd. Powys re-emerged as a separate realm only after the death of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn in 1063, under Bleddyn ap Cynfyn, who was killed in 1075. His sons Cadwgan, Iorwerth and Maredudd fought one another and the Norman lords of the March, notably Roger of Montgomery and his son Robert of Bellême, earls of Shrewsbury. Family feuds and royal interference repeatedly weakened the dynasty. Maredudd ap Bleddyn restored a measure of unity before his death in 1132, and his son Madog ap Maredudd made Powys a significant power again, bringing Welsh troops to fight for Ranulf, Earl of Chester, at the Battle of Lincoln in 1141 and allying with Henry II against Owain Gwynedd in 1157. When Madog died in 1160, followed soon after by his son and heir Llywelyn, the kingdom was divided among his kin. Northern Powys became Powys Fadog under Gruffudd Maelor, and southern Powys became Powys Wenwynwyn, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#owain-cyfeiliog">Owain Cyfeiliog</a>’s son Gwenwynwyn. Both lost their independence in the Edwardian conquest of 1277–1283, Powys Wenwynwyn surviving as the marcher lordship of Powis.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Powys was the Welsh kingdom that stood longest on the frontier with Mercia and later the Norman March, losing its lowlands to the English but leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a>, a rich body of poetry and a dynasty that revived under Bleddyn ap Cynfyn and Madog ap Maredudd before splitting in 1160.</em></p>

